There is a HUGE difference between "porting" and merely "configuring and 
compiling", Josh. "Porting" means that the product has been successfully 
compiled and tested on a given platform, and runs without any known problems 
on that platform. It can often be VERY difficult and time-consuming to port 
programs (especially ones of the size and complexity of NetHack!) to a given
platform. 

"Configuring and compiling", on the other hand, is just the process of 
transforming source into an executable (roughly speaking). It's usually MUCH 
simpler than "porting". In fact, by calling the *nix port "unsupported",
it's almost like a (unintentional, I'm sure) putdown of the long years of
work which has been invested in the *nix portability of the game, and in the
ease-of-configuration of those *nix ports. 

