In the case of NetHack, there is no "vendor", per se, so that's one technical
little nit-pick right off the bat (is that a mixed metaphor? :-)

In a deeper sense, it is not particularly practical to supply binaries of the 
various *nix "ports" of NetHack - the code in its current form requires that
certain site-specific elements be COMPILED INTO the executable. Pathnames,
loginid's, etc. It cannot be assumed, for example, that everyone puts the
"nethack" script in the same place. Some put it in "/usr/games", others in
"/usr/games/bin", others in "/usr/games/lib", others in "/usr/local/bin", etc.,
etc., etc.. Some folks even install and run the game only in their $HOME 
directory-structure. Such things HAVE to be left up to the discretion of 
whoever configures the game. On PC's and such, generally the games-admin has 
unlimited power to change his or her machine to accommodate the binary. The 
same is usually true wrt a commercial *nix product. But for NetHack on *nix, 
the games-admin often does not have that luxury. Maybe one day all ports of 
NetHack can be driven -completely- by a local config file consulted at run-
time, but that is not the case currently.

>>The Unix version is what all the other ports are based on.  The VMS port
>>is as "official" as any of the others.  Binaries are provided for the
>>microcomputer versions because of the number of people who wouldn't be
>>able to build them otherwise.  People on multi-user systems are expected
>>to build from sources themselves.  VMS binaries happen to be available
>>at an alternate site.

Exactly. The number of *nix machines which lack a C compiler of any kind are 
few and far between. Why "straightjacket" people with a binary (which has 
frozen loginid's, pathnames, etc.) when you can give them source, which 
encapsulates all of your hard porting work, and they can just configure the 
site-specific stuff, compile and run?? That way, they can apply subsequent 
patches too, so they can keep NetHack up-to-date indefinitely, instead of 
being frozen at a certain release and patch level. The presence of source also 
stimulates many people to get interested in the internal workings of the game, 
to figure out their own "spoilers" (if they want), to find bugs, suggest 
enhancements, etc.. Source has many benefits that binaries lack. And there's 
usually nothing (except perhaps network connectivity/bandwidth :-) to stop 
game-admins with similar machines from exchanging binaries with each other, or 
making them generally-available via FTP. On the other hand, Many PC's (etc.) 
don't have C compilers and/or the user is unable or unwilling to run a C 
compiler. So binaries often make more sense in that environment. The Law of 
Supply & Demand has thus dictated that binaries for PC's and other 
"single-user" machines be made available in central repositories. That same 
Law has dictated that *nix ports are generally happy with having only source 
available in central repositories. Who are we mere mortals to break the 
immutable Law of Supply & Demand, eh? :-) 

